  Public surface is two functions: [38;5;153mgetTrackingId()[39m → [38;5;153mstring | null[39m, and [38;5;153mclearTrackingId()[39m → [38;5;153mvoid[39m.

  [1mOne decision inside this section I want your call on.[0m The natural ID generator is [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m, but it's only
  defined in a [3msecure context[0m. [38;5;153mfile://[39m qualifies in Chrome and Firefox, so your common case is fine — but plain [38;5;153mhttp://[39m
  on a LAN host does not, and there [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID[39m is [38;5;153mundefined[39m. That's a third failure mode distinct from the
  storage one you already decided, and it needs its own answer: storage works, ID generation doesn't.

  Returning [38;5;153mnull[39m there is the consistent reading of "null means no durable ID" — but it's arguably the [3mwrong[0m
  consistency, because storage is fine and we genuinely could persist something. A [38;5;153mMath.random[39m-based ID is not
  cryptographically random, which would matter for a session token and does not matter here: a correlation ID needs
  collision resistance, not unpredictability, and it is never used for authorization. Falling back keeps the
  cross-page-load linkage you asked for on insecure origins instead of silently dropping the feature there.

  My recommendation is the fallback, with a comment stating plainly that this value is a correlation ID and must never
  be used as a secret — because the real risk of a [38;5;153mMath.random[39m ID in a codebase is someone later mistaking it for one.

  [1mAlso flagged, not asking:[0m [38;5;153mclearTrackingId()[39m will exist but have no caller — the app has no logout. I'm not adding one;
  that's scope creep. The design notes it as an explicit gap so the reset path gets wired when logout arrives.
